//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// A region placed on the host, with a loop in it, that compiles and runs.
//
// This was a fail test. `spawn on(Topology::CPU)` was lowered to
// `async.execute`, whose body the async dialect requires to be a single block,
// and a `for` is four -- so a host region carrying any control flow was
// rejected, naming the async op the program never wrote. That file also
// recorded a second gap: nothing lowered `async.execute` either, and
// `mlir-translate` does not register the async dialect, so even a single-block
// host region failed to translate. Every `spawn on(Topology::CPU)` was
// unrunnable.
//
// Both had one cause. A spawn states where a region belongs; for the host there
// is nothing to dispatch to, and the placement is a declaration the checker
// enforces rather than a request for concurrency. The region is inlined where
// it was written -- the parent block splits around it, its blocks land in
// between, and each `vx.yield` becomes a branch to the continuation -- so a
// loop or a branch inside it is ordinary code.
//
// What this pins is that the host half of a two-device program can be spelled.
// A program that puts a matmul on the Neural Engine and the rest on the CPU
// says both placements only if this compiles; otherwise the host half has to be
// bare code and the program names one device while using two.
//
// The accumulator starts non-zero so that "the loop did not run" and "the
// tensor was never written" are different answers.
//
// RUN: vxc %s --action emit-mlir 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK-LABEL: func.func @main
// CHECK: vx.spawn topology(0)
// EXPECT: 4.5

fn main() -> i32 {
  let mut a = Tensor<f32, [ 1, 1 ]>::new();
  a[0][0] = 1.5;
  spawn on(Topology::CPU) {
    for _i in 0..3 {
      a[0][0] = a[0][0] + 1.0;
    }
  }
  print(a[0][0]);
  return 0;
}
